finding: Acquisition failure is detected by texture.width/height, not by the scoped validation result (src/core/rendering/view.ts:426-434). Invalid textures need not be zero-sized. Independently reproduced in installed headless Chromium with --enable-unsafe-webgpu: configure a canvas whose width is device.limits.maxTextureDimension2D+1 and height 200, scope getCurrentTexture(), then scope texture.createView(). Acquisition returns width 8193, height 200 and validation 'Texture size ... exceeded maximum texture size ... 8192'; createView generates '[Invalid Texture] is invalid due to a previous error'. acquireCanvasTexture therefore returns this invalid texture immediately; substrate.ts:494-496 calls createView outside its scope before the recovery callback can run, triggering the fatal uncaptured error rather than continuing. This is the unit's explicit over-limit acquisition-failure model on another browser. surface.test.ts hardcodes invalid={width:0,height:0}, so all five tests pass while this claim fails. A failed acquisition must not reach downstream consumers merely because its invalid texture has positive dimensions; add coverage using a positive-dimension invalid texture and the presenting frame.
